Subject: [PATCH 3/5] LOCAL ONLY: do not pulse soc_rst at probe

NOT for upstream. Bit 0 of the SoCController reset register resets the full SoC, and on the hcfpga
designs the PCIe endpoint is part of that SoC. Probe therefore pulses a reset through the link it is
talking over, waits 10 ms, and immediately reads 256 words back. On an AMD host a PCIe completion
timeout is fatal: the machine resets with no oops and nothing in the journal.

The framing reset that matters is a different register in the algorithm block, which the bridge
asserts itself while the DMA engines are stopped.

--- a/main.c
+++ b/main.c
@@ -1492,8 +1492,25 @@
 		goto fail1;
 	}
 
-	/* Reset LitePCIe core */
-#ifdef CSR_CTRL_RESET_ADDR
+	/* Reset LitePCIe core.
+	 *
+	 * DELIBERATELY DISABLED FOR THE HCFPGA DESIGNS, and this is a LOCAL divergence that is NOT part of
+	 * the one-shot patch we would propose upstream. The change is 0003-local-disable-soc-reset.patch.
+	 *
+	 * Bit 0 of the SoCController reset register is soc_rst, "reset the full SoC (pulse reset)" in
+	 * LiteX's own words. On our designs the PCIe endpoint is part of that SoC, so this pulses a reset
+	 * through the very link the driver is talking over, waits a fixed 10 ms, and then immediately reads
+	 * 256 words of the identifier back through it. If the endpoint has not finished recovering, those
+	 * reads hit a dead link. On an AMD host a PCIe completion timeout is fatal: the machine resets
+	 * instantly with no oops, no trace and nothing flushed to the journal.
+	 *
+	 * We do not need it. The framing reset that actually matters is a different register, ctrl bit 0 of
+	 * the ALGORITHM block at offset 0x00, which the bridge asserts itself while the DMA engines are
+	 * stopped. That resets the feeder, the collector and the cores without touching the endpoint.
+	 *
+	 * Do not restore this without a way to re-establish the link afterwards.
+	 */
+#if 0
 	litepcie_writel(litepcie_dev, CSR_CTRL_RESET_ADDR, 1);
 	msleep(10);
 #endif
